package com.thanatos.recoveryapp;

public class LessonStore2 {
    public static String get(String topic) {
        if (topic == null) return null;
        switch (topic) {

            case "Neuroplasticity":
                return "NEUROPLASTICITY\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT IS\nNeuroplasticity is your brain's ability to change its connections in response to experience. It is the reason habits form, and the reason they can be rebuilt.\n\n"
                    + "IN THE BRAIN\nNeurons talk across connections called synapses. When two neurons are repeatedly active together, the connection between them tends to get stronger (long-term potentiation), and connections that go unused tend to weaken or get pruned. This is the idea behind the saying 'neurons that fire together wire together.' Over time, repeated thoughts, actions, and experiences shape which pathways are fast and easy.\n\n"
                    + "Real examples: London taxi drivers who memorized the city's streets had a larger posterior hippocampus than controls, and people who learned to juggle showed gray matter changes within a few months. Whether adults grow many new neurons is still debated. The evidence for strengthening and reorganizing existing connections is much stronger.\n\n"
                    + "WHY IT MATTERS IN RECOVERY\nAddiction is plasticity at work: repeated use strengthens cue and craving pathways. Recovery uses the same mechanism in the other direction. Each time you practice a new response, you strengthen a competing pathway, and the old one gets less use. Imaging studies of people who stay abstinent show partial recovery in brain structure and function over months to years, though timelines vary a lot.\n\n"
                    + "WHAT HELPS PLASTICITY\nRepetition, focused attention, emotional meaning, sleep (which helps consolidate learning), and exercise. Short, frequent, slightly challenging practice beats occasional marathons.\n\n"
                    + "TRY THIS\n1. Pick one small skill (slow breathing, labeling a feeling) and do it daily for two weeks.\n2. Do it at the same time and place so it links to a cue.\n3. Give it your full attention. Autopilot reps teach less.\n4. Protect your sleep. It is when the day's learning gets locked in.\n5. Track your reps in your journal. Seeing a streak is its own reward.";

            case "The Habit Loop":
                return "THE HABIT LOOP\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT IS\nA habit is a behavior your brain runs automatically once a familiar cue shows up. The basic loop is cue, routine, reward.\n\n"
                    + "IN THE BRAIN\nEarly on, behavior is goal-directed and leans heavily on the prefrontal cortex and the dorsomedial striatum. With repetition, control shifts toward the dorsolateral striatum, part of the basal ganglia, which stores action sequences as automatic chunks. That saves mental effort, which is why habits feel automatic. Addiction is thought to push this process further: drug cues become very strong triggers, and the behavior gets harder to stop even when you want to.\n\n"
                    + "Old habits are not deleted. New learning forms a competing memory that can win out, but the old pattern can return, especially under stress or back in the original setting. That is why plans for risky places and times matter.\n\n"
                    + "WHY IT MATTERS IN RECOVERY\nYou can't always think your way out of a habit in the moment, because it runs below conscious thought. What works is changing the setup around it: the cue, the routine, and the reward.\n\n"
                    + "TRY THIS\n1. Map one loop: what cue (time, place, person, feeling), what routine, and what reward did you get (relief, numbness, company, a break)?\n2. Keep the reward, swap the routine. If the payoff was relief, pick a faster relief that fits recovery: a walk, a call, a shower.\n3. Make an if-then plan: 'If it's 9pm and I'm alone, then I text someone and go for a walk.' Pre-made plans cut the decision load.\n4. Change the environment: remove cues and add friction to the old route.\n5. Be patient. One study found new habits took about two months on average to feel automatic, with a huge range (roughly 18 to 254 days).";

            case "Amygdala and Prefrontal Cortex":
                return "AMYGDALA AND PREFRONTAL CORTEX\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT IS\nTwo key players in emotion regulation. The amygdala flags what matters or threatens you, fast. The prefrontal cortex helps you evaluate, plan, and decide how to respond.\n\n"
                    + "IN THE BRAIN\nThe amygdala reacts to threats and emotionally important cues within fractions of a second, often before you know why. Prefrontal regions, including the ventromedial and lateral prefrontal cortex, connect back to the amygdala and can turn its response up or down depending on how you interpret the situation. Stronger connectivity between them is associated with better emotion regulation.\n\n"
                    + "It is not a simple fight between a primitive part and a rational part. These regions work as a network, and the amygdala is also active in positive and uncertain situations. But the practical picture holds: when alarm is high and prefrontal function is low, reactions run the show.\n\n"
                    + "Stress is the main switch. Acute stress floods the prefrontal cortex with catecholamines (norepinephrine and dopamine) that impair working memory and self-control, while strengthening the amygdala and habit circuits. Sleep loss pushes in the same direction. Chronic substance use tends to make the amygdala more reactive and weaken prefrontal control, which is part of why early recovery can feel emotionally intense. Both tend to improve with time.\n\n"
                    + "WHY IT MATTERS IN RECOVERY\nWhen you're flooded, you haven't lost your intelligence. The prefrontal brakes are temporarily offline. Calm the alarm first, then use the thinking tools.\n\n"
                    + "TRY THIS\n1. Lower arousal first: slow breathing or a brisk walk for a few minutes.\n2. Label the emotion in specific words.\n3. Reappraise: what else could this situation mean?\n4. Delay big decisions until you're calm.\n5. Protect sleep, meals, and downtime, since they keep the prefrontal side working.";

            case "Dopamine and Reward":
                return "DOPAMINE AND REWARD\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT IS\nDopamine is a chemical messenger tied to motivation, learning, and wanting. It is not simply the pleasure chemical.\n\n"
                    + "IN THE BRAIN\nDopamine neurons in the midbrain fire most for rewards that are better than expected, and dip below baseline when an expected reward doesn't arrive. This is called reward prediction error, and it is how the brain learns what is worth pursuing. After learning, dopamine fires to the cue that predicts the reward rather than the reward itself, which is why cues can create such strong wanting.\n\n"
                    + "Most addictive substances raise dopamine in the nucleus accumbens far more, and faster, than natural rewards like food or music. Researchers describe incentive sensitization: the wanting system becomes hypersensitive to drug cues while actual liking can fade, so a person can crave something that no longer feels very good. With repeated use the brain also adapts, and many imaging studies show lower D2 receptor availability, so everyday pleasures can feel flat.\n\n"
                    + "WHY IT MATTERS IN RECOVERY\nEarly flatness is a brain recalibrating, not proof that life will always feel this way. Imaging studies show dopamine function partly recovering with sustained abstinence over months. Natural rewards come back slowly, so you often have to start an activity before the enjoyment catches up.\n\n"
                    + "TRY THIS\n1. Schedule small, doable rewards every day: a favorite meal, music, a shower, time outside.\n2. Try behavioral activation: do the activity first, then rate the enjoyment 0 to 10. People often find it higher than they predicted.\n3. Break big goals into small steps so you get frequent wins. The brain learns from them.\n4. Use exercise and sleep as steady support for the reward system.\n5. Be wary of quick fixes. Claims about 'dopamine fasting' don't have solid evidence behind them. Steady routines do.";

            case "Stress and the HPA Axis":
                return "STRESS AND THE HPA AXIS\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT IS\nThe body's stress chain of command, and one of the biggest drivers of craving and relapse.\n\n"
                    + "IN THE BRAIN\nWhen the brain detects a threat, the amygdala signals the hypothalamus, which releases CRH. That tells the pituitary to release ACTH, which tells the adrenal glands to release cortisol. This chain is the HPA axis. A faster system runs alongside it: the sympathetic nervous system releases adrenaline within seconds (racing heart, fast breathing), while cortisol rises over minutes and lasts longer. Normally cortisol feeds back to the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex to shut the response off.\n\n"
                    + "Chronic stress and repeated substance use can disrupt that feedback. In studies of people in early recovery, stress exposure increased craving and was linked to a higher risk of relapse. Chronic stress is also associated with changes in the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex, though these effects are often at least partly reversible.\n\n"
                    + "WHY IT MATTERS IN RECOVERY\nStress cravings are different from cue cravings. You may have no obvious trigger, just a rough day. Knowing that means you treat the stress, not only the urge.\n\n"
                    + "TRY THIS\n1. Use slow breathing in the first few minutes of a stress spike.\n2. Move: regular exercise lowers stress reactivity over time.\n3. Guard your sleep. Poor sleep amplifies the stress response.\n4. Talk to someone. Social support buffers stress.\n5. Schedule downtime every day (music, nature, a hobby), and don't wait until you're maxed out.\n6. Mindfulness-based programs reduce stress reactivity in some studies, though the evidence on cortisol specifically is mixed.";

            default:
                return LessonStore3.get(topic);
        }
    }
}
